{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`LLMConfig` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:473`) exposes `base_url` and `api_key` but no way to\nattach request headers, so an operator behind a gateway cannot label rebar's gate calls. A raw\ndict is unsafe as-is: both vendor SDKs merge caller headers last, so a caller-supplied\n`Authorization` silently displaces the real credential. This ticket adds the configuration\nsurface and the validation that makes it safe; nothing is sent yet.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `headers: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)` to `LLMConfig`, resolved in\n`from_env` by the three-layer precedence `mcp_servers` already uses at `config.py:574-594`\n(CLI `-c llm.headers=<json>` > `REBAR_LLM_HEADERS` > the `[tool.rebar.llm]` file table).\n\n**Precedence only — not error handling.** `mcp_servers` degrades malformed JSON to `{}`\nsilently in all three layers (`config.py:582-583`, `:593-594`). `headers` raises\n`LLMConfigError` naming the layer instead, in every layer. The file layer accepts a native TOML\ntable or a JSON string (mirroring `:585-592`); a malformed string, a value that is neither, or\nJSON parsing to a non-object is a hard error. Silently discarding headers would leave an\noperator with an unattributed gateway and no signal.\n\nGrammar and validation live in a new leaf module `src/rebar/llm/headers.py`, testable without\nbuilding a config.\n\n**The `${...}` grammar is closed.** Two namespaces, `env` and `run`; `headers.py` owns that set\nand the `${run:...}` key vocabulary (`trace_id`, `ticket_id`, `operation`) as a module constant,\nso the grammar does not depend on the ticket supplying those values. `$$` escapes to a literal\n`$` anywhere in a value — the OpenTelemetry Collector rule, adopted whole so an implementer who\nknows that convention cannot generalize it differently; `$${VAR}` yields `${VAR}` unsubstituted.\nOtherwise any `${` begins a placeholder, dispositioned at config-resolution time:\n\n- `${env:VAR}` — resolved now; unset is a hard error, matching the `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` rule\n  that a missing path never silently degrades (`config.py:19-21`).\n- `${run:KEY}` — left unresolved (its value exists only inside a gate run), but `KEY` is checked\n  against the closed vocabulary now, so a typo fails at configuration time.\n- Unrecognized namespace (`${foo:bar}`), missing colon (`${envVAR}`), or unclosed `${...}` —\n  hard `LLMConfigError`, never a silently-accepted literal, since passing placeholder text\n  through as a header value would be a silent misconfiguration.\n\n**Ordering: substitute first, then validate.** Every check runs on the resolved value, never on\na literal containing `${env:...}`. Validating first would let an env var whose value holds CR/LF\ncarry an injected header past the check meant to stop it — a configured `${env:GATEWAY_TAG}`\nwhere `GATEWAY_TAG` is `foo\\r\\nInjected: bar` passes a check applied to the literal, and\nsubstitution then injects. Validation runs after `${env:...}` substitution with any `${run:...}`\nplaceholder still present as literal text (which holds no control characters); the delivery\nticket re-runs it after substituting those.\n\nRejected with a named `LLMConfigError`: the header names `authorization`, `x-api-key`, `cookie`,\n`host`, `proxy-authorization` (case-insensitive, exact-name); names outside RFC 7230 token\ncharacters; values containing CR, LF or NUL. The error names `llm.api_key` as the supported\nchannel, so the denylist redirects to a first-class field rather than merely forbidding — the\nposture Prometheus takes, whose `ReservedHeaders` denylist is legitimate because it ships\ndedicated auth blocks. A denylist rather than an allowlist because arbitrary gateway header\nnames are the point, and the threats closed — credential displacement and protocol injection —\nare themselves closed sets.\n\nDocumentation for this surface is delivered by sibling `98b6-efa8-bf15-4760`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the `headers` field and its three-layer resolution in `from_env`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/headers.py` (new) — grammar, `${env:...}` resolution, name/value validation.\n- `tests/unit/test_llm_headers.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests against the real functions — pure string resolution and validation,\nno boundary to cross.\n\n- Happy path: a literal header and an `${env:VAR}` header both resolve.\n- Grammar dispositions: `${foo:bar}`, `${envVAR}`, `${en:VAR}`, `${env:VAR`, `${run:bogus}` each\n  raise; a well-formed `${run:trace_id}` survives unresolved; `$${VAR}` and `a$$b` escape.\n- Failure paths: each denied name; an invalid name character; CR, LF and NUL in values; an\n  `${env:VAR}` naming an unset variable; malformed JSON in each of the three layers; a\n  file-table value that is neither a table nor a string.\n- Injection-via-indirection: a value of `${env:VAR}` where `VAR` resolves to a control character\n  is rejected — the ordering regression test.\n- Negative control: `x-authorization-context` accepted, proving exact-name matching.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `LLMConfig.headers` defaults to `{}` so an unconfigured deployment is byte-unchanged.\n- [ ] Headers resolve from all three layers with CLI > env > file precedence; checked by setting\n      all three and asserting the CLI value wins.\n- [ ] A `${env:VAR}` value resolves from the environment; checked by a monkeypatched test.\n- [ ] An `${env:VAR}` naming an unset variable raises `LLMConfigError` naming the variable.\n- [ ] An unrecognized namespace, missing colon, unclosed placeholder, or `${run:...}` key outside\n      the closed vocabulary each raise `LLMConfigError` rather than being accepted as a literal;\n      checked by a parametrized test over the five forms above.\n- [ ] A well-formed `${run:trace_id}` survives config resolution unresolved; checked by asserting\n      the stored value still contains the placeholder.\n- [ ] `$$` resolves to a literal `$` anywhere in a value, so `$${VAR}` yields `${VAR}`\n      unsubstituted and `a$$b` yields `a$b`; checked by a parametrized test over both shapes.\n- [ ] A value clean as a literal but resolving through `${env:VAR}` to a string containing CR,\n      LF or NUL is rejected; checked by a parametrized test over the three characters, proving\n      validation runs on the resolved value.\n- [ ] Each denied name is rejected case-insensitively; checked by a parametrized test over the\n      five names in three casings.\n- [ ] The rejection message for a denied name names `llm.api_key`.\n- [ ] Malformed JSON raises `LLMConfigError` naming the layer rather than degrading to `{}`, in\n      all three layers; checked by a parametrized test over env, CLI and the file table.\n- [ ] A file-table value that is neither a table nor a string, or a JSON string parsing to a\n      non-object, raises `LLMConfigError`.\n- [ ] No gateway-vendor header name appears as a string constant under `src/rebar/llm/`; checked\n      by a test scanning that tree — excluding its own file — for the case-insensitive\n      HEADER-SHAPED literals `x-litellm-`, `helicone-` and `x-langfuse-`, each verified to occur\n      zero times today. Bare `langfuse_` is deliberately not scanned: it occurs 42 times across\n      10 files as rebar's own legitimate vocabulary (`langfuse_prompt`, `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY`),\n      so scanning it would fail on day one.\n- [ ] `x-authorization-context` is accepted; checked by a test asserting it survives."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787725344375876001,"uuid":"ab94dc53-7465-4ee4-ab04-f3ecbff2ee8f"}